const a="day-trips-from-tirana-by-car",t="8 Best Day Trips from Tirana by Car: Castles, Lakes, Mountains and the Sea",e="Day Trips from Tirana by Car: 8 Best Destinations",r="The best day trips from Tirana by car: Krujë, Durrës, Berat, Shkodër, Lake Bovilla, Dajti, Lake Ohrid and Elbasan, with driving times and parking tips.",o="Eight easy and rewarding day trips from Tirana by car, with approximate driving times, highlights and practical parking tips for each destination.",n=["day trips from Tirana by car","day trip Tirana to Berat","Tirana to Kruja by car","Tirana to Shkodër day trip","Lake Bovilla by car","things to do near Tirana","Tirana to Lake Ohrid by car"],h=6,i=`<p>Tirana is an excellent base for day trips by car. Within about an hour you can reach the castle town of Krujë, the beaches of Durrës or the cable car on Mount Dajti, and within two to three hours you can explore Berat, Shkodër or even Lake Ohrid. Here are eight of the best day trips from Tirana, with approximate driving times, what to see and where to park.</p><h2>Quick overview: driving times from Tirana</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Approx. driving time (one way)</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Krujë</td><td>About 45 minutes to 1 hour</td><td>History, bazaar, views</td></tr><tr><td>Durrës</td><td>About 40 minutes to 1 hour</td><td>Roman ruins, beach, seafood</td></tr><tr><td>Mount Dajti</td><td>About 30 minutes to 1 hour</td><td>Mountain views, cable car, walks</td></tr><tr><td>Elbasan</td><td>About 1 hour</td><td>Castle walls, old town</td></tr><tr><td>Lake Bovilla</td><td>About 1 to 1.5 hours</td><td>Hiking, turquoise water</td></tr><tr><td>Shkodër</td><td>About 1.5 to 2 hours</td><td>Rozafa Castle, lake, culture</td></tr><tr><td>Berat</td><td>About 1.5 to 2 hours</td><td>UNESCO old town, castle</td></tr><tr><td>Pogradec / Lake Ohrid</td><td>About 2.5 to 3 hours</td><td>Lake scenery, a long day out</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Times depend on traffic, especially when leaving Tirana at rush hour and on summer weekends. Start early to make the most of the day.</p><h2>1. Krujë: Skanderbeg's mountain town</h2><p>Perched on a mountainside north of Tirana, Krujë is the town most associated with Skanderbeg, Albania's national hero. Walk up the cobbled <strong>old bazaar</strong>, lined with shops selling carpets, crafts and antiques, then visit the castle area and the Skanderbeg Museum. The views over the plain towards the coast are superb on a clear day.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> Park in one of the car parks near the start of the bazaar rather than driving up the narrow lanes. Weekends get busy, so arrive in the morning.</p><h2>2. Durrës: Roman history by the sea</h2><p>Albania's main port city is the quickest way to reach the Adriatic from Tirana. See the <strong>Roman amphitheatre</strong>, one of the largest in the Balkans, the Venetian tower and the seafront promenade. In summer, the long sandy beach south of the city fills with locals escaping the heat.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> Central Durrës has paid street parking and private car parks. Park once and explore the old centre on foot. Expect heavy traffic on the coastal road on summer weekends.</p><h2>3. Mount Dajti: Tirana's mountain</h2><p>Dajti rises right behind Tirana, so it is the easiest escape from the city. You can drive up the winding mountain road or drive to the lower station of the <strong>Dajti Ekspres cable car</strong> on the edge of the city and ride up. At the top you will find walking paths, restaurants with panoramic terraces and cool air even in summer.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> There is parking near the lower cable car station. If you drive all the way up, take the bends slowly and watch for oncoming traffic.</p><h2>4. Elbasan: an old town inside castle walls</h2><p>Elbasan, in central Albania, is less visited by tourists, which is part of its charm. Its historic centre sits inside well-preserved <strong>castle walls</strong>, with narrow streets, cafés and an old church and mosque within the fortress. It combines well with a drive towards Lake Ohrid if you want to break up a longer route.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> Park outside the walls and walk in. The old town is compact and best explored on foot.</p><h2>5. Lake Bovilla: turquoise water and a classic hike</h2><p>Lake Bovilla is a reservoir in the mountains north-east of Tirana, famous for its bright turquoise water. The most popular activity is the hike up to the <strong>Gamti viewpoint</strong>, which overlooks the lake and the surrounding peaks. Bring water, good shoes and sun protection.</p><p><strong>Road tip:</strong> The last part of the road can be narrow and rough in places. Drive slowly, check current conditions before you go and consider an <a href="/makina-suv-me-qira">SUV</a> for extra ground clearance. Avoid the trip after heavy rain.</p><h2>6. Shkodër and Rozafa Castle</h2><p>Shkodër, in the north, is one of Albania's oldest cities and a cultural centre. The highlight is <strong>Rozafa Castle</strong>, standing on a hill above the meeting point of rivers and Lake Shkodër, with a fine view and a famous legend. In town, stroll the pedestrian street, visit the Marubi photography museum and enjoy a lakeside lunch.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> Rozafa has parking near the castle entrance. In the centre, use paid car parks and walk or cycle; Shkodër is one of Albania's most bike-friendly cities.</p><p>Shkodër is also the gateway to the Albanian Alps. If you want to go further, read our guide to <a href="/blog/theth-valbona-by-car">driving to Theth and Valbona</a>.</p><h2>7. Berat: the city of a thousand windows</h2><p>Berat is a UNESCO World Heritage site and one of the most beautiful towns in Albania. Admire the white Ottoman-era houses of the <strong>Mangalem</strong> quarter climbing the hillside, cross the river to Gorica, and walk up to the <strong>castle</strong>, where people still live inside the walls. The Onufri Museum, with its icons, is worth a visit.</p><p><strong>Parking tip:</strong> Park in the lower town and walk up to the castle. The road to the top is steep and narrow, and parking there is limited in high season.</p><h2>8. Pogradec and Lake Ohrid: a long but rewarding day</h2><p>Lake Ohrid, shared by Albania and North Macedonia, is one of the oldest lakes in Europe. On the Albanian side, the lakeside town of <strong>Pogradec</strong> has a pleasant promenade, and the nearby village of <strong>Lin</strong> sits on a small peninsula with lovely views. It is a long day, so start early and plan to be back after dark.</p><p>If you would like to visit Ohrid town or St. Naum on the Macedonian side, you will need cross-border permission for your rental car. See our guide to <a href="/blog/crossing-borders-rental-car-albania">crossing borders with a rental car</a>.</p><h2>Practical tips for day trips from Tirana</h2><ul><li><strong>Leave early.</strong> Traffic out of Tirana builds quickly in the morning and on summer weekends.</li><li><strong>Carry some cash.</strong> Small car parks, cafés and entrance fees outside the big cities may be cash only.</li><li><strong>Use offline maps.</strong> Mobile coverage can be weak in the mountains.</li><li><strong>Drive in daylight</strong> on mountain roads whenever possible.</li><li><strong>Keep the tank topped up</strong> before heading into rural areas.</li><li><strong>Never leave valuables visible</strong> in a parked car.</li></ul><p>New to Albanian roads? Take it slowly on your first day, and remember that local driving habits can feel more spontaneous than at home.</p><h2>Rent a car in Tirana for your day trips</h2><p>Day trips are the best reason to have your own car in Tirana. Rent Ride delivers free of charge in <a href="/makina-me-qira-tirane">central Tirana</a> and at Tirana Airport, with prices from €25/day, no security deposit and 24/7 support. <a href="/rezervo">Book your car online</a> in a couple of minutes.</p>`,s=[{question:"What is the best day trip from Tirana by car?",answer:"For history and atmosphere, Berat is hard to beat, with its UNESCO-listed old town and lived-in castle, about 1.5 to 2 hours away. If you want something shorter, Krujë is under an hour from Tirana and combines a historic bazaar with mountain views. Shkodër is ideal if you also want a lake."},{question:"Can I visit Berat and back in one day from Tirana?",answer:"Yes. Berat is about 1.5 to 2 hours from Tirana each way, so a day trip is comfortable. Leave in the morning, explore the Mangalem quarter and the castle, have lunch in town and drive back in the late afternoon. Allow extra time on summer weekends when traffic is heavier."},{question:"Do I need an SUV to reach Lake Bovilla?",answer:"Not always, but the final stretch of the road can be narrow and rough, so a car with more ground clearance makes the trip more comfortable. Check current conditions before you go, drive slowly and avoid the trip after heavy rain. Ask your rental company whether the route is suitable for your car."},{question:"Is Lake Ohrid too far for a day trip from Tirana?",answer:"It is possible, but it is a long day. Pogradec, on the Albanian shore, is roughly 2.5 to 3 hours away by car each way. Start early, keep the plan simple and consider staying overnight if you also want to visit the Macedonian side, which requires cross-border permission for a rental car."}],d={slug:a,title:t,metaTitle:e,metaDescription:r,excerpt:o,keywords:n,readingMinutes:6,html:i,faq:s};export{d as default,o as excerpt,s as faq,i as html,n as keywords,r as metaDescription,e as metaTitle,h as readingMinutes,a as slug,t as title};
